WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.167
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome
to the Huberman Lab podcast

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.850
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:05.630
for everyday life.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.120
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.100
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.900
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.840
Today we are discussing ways
to improve your fitness.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.700
In particular we are
going to discuss tools

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.890
that you can incorporate
into your existing fitness

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.900
routine that will
allow you to make

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.970
significant improvement
without having

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.835
to invest a lot of extra time.

00:00:27.835 --> 00:00:29.960
Most all of the tools we
are going to discuss today

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.850
were gleaned from the
6 episodes that we

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.010
did with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.650
We provide a link to those
full episodes in the show note

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.610
captions, of course.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.680
Now, those episodes
included a very large number

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.010
of protocols, everything from
how to build a fitness, routine

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.100
how to enhance recovery,
nutrition and supplementation,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.640
exercises and routines
aimed specifically

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.040
at strength or
hypertrophy or endurance

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.230
or building anaerobic capacity.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.410
What I've done is to select key
protocols from those episodes

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.510
that I myself have
started to incorporate

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.330
into my existing fitness
routine and that I

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.510
think will be especially
beneficial and frankly fun

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.130
for you to incorporate
into your fitness routine.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.810
Now, a little bit
later in this episode,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.140
I review the key components
of any fitness program, that

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.080
is the number and type of
cardiovascular training

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.940
sessions and resistance
training sessions

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.370
that are essential for
everyone to include

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.610
as a template or a foundation
for their overall fitness

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:24.120
program.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.745
Now, a little bit
later in the episode,

00:01:25.745 --> 00:01:27.270
I will be sure to
review what are

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.000
the essential components
of any fitness program,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.860
so the number and type of
resistance training sessions,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.980
the number and type of
cardiovascular training

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.050
sessions, as well as
some of the elements

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.030
of how those are
arranged to ensure

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.400
proper and adequate
recovery between sessions

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.170
so that you can continue
to make ongoing progress.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.140
However, the bulk of
today's discussion

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.710
is going to focus on tools
that you can use again very

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.190
easily, very quickly, in
some cases even saving you

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.100
time during your
fitness regimen in order

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.110
to improve all aspects of
your fitness, your endurance,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.290
your muscular endurance,
your anaerobic capacity,

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.660
your recovery, your
strength, your hypertrophy.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.570
And in describing these tools
to improve your fitness,

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.060
it also provides an opportunity
for each and all of us

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.580
to step back from our
existing fitness routine

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.770
and ask whether or not
it's really checking off

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.450
all the boxes that
are necessary,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.360
as well as where we can be
more economical with our time

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.950
and our efforts
in order to reach

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.260
our specific goals related
to exercise and performance.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.770
So by the end of
today's episode,

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.520
you can be sure that
you have at least one

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.010
and as many as 12 tools
that you can incorporate

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.660
into your existing
fitness routine,

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.900
again, without adding much
additional time or effort that

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.910
are sure to accelerates
your progress.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.770
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.260
that this podcast is separate
from my teaching and research

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.310
roles at Stanford.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.440
It is however part of
my desire and effort

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.360
to bring zero cost to
consumer information

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.310
about science and
science-related tools

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.540
to the general public.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.040
In keeping with
that theme, I'd like

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.450
to thank the sponsors
of today's podcast.

