00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.810
Let's talk about tools
to improve your fitness.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.760
Before we do that,
however, I just

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.950
want to briefly
remind everybody what

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:24.640
constitutes a core or a
foundational fitness program.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.590
Now, what I'm about
to describe is not

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.260
for the athlete that's
trying to just improve

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:32.390
one aspect of fitness
or sports performance.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.600
So for instance, if
you are a powerlifter

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.210
and your main goal is
to move more weight

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.460
on the core
powerlifting movements,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.580
or if you are somebody
training for a marathon,

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.040
it's likely that
your core fitness

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.110
program will differ
substantially from what

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:48.250
I'm about to describe.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.590
However, the vast
majority of you

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.990
are almost certainly
trying to have some level

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:54.620
of cardiovascular fitness.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.670
So the ability perhaps
to run a mile or more,

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.800
certainly to be able to
walk up a flight of stairs

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.170
without getting winded,
you almost certainly want

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.060
some degree of
strength, the ability

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.880
to perhaps pick up a
heavy load of groceries

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.700
and carry it in one arm
as you carry something

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:10.750
else in the other arm.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:12.970
You want the ability
to help move furniture.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.760
You want the ability certainly
to not injure yourself

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.273
when performing daily
tasks, and perhaps

00:06:18.273 --> 00:06:20.440
you also want to be able
to go out and play a pickup

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.380
game of basketball or
soccer, or to go out

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:26.320
on a long hike with a family
without feeling so sore

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.360
that you have to rest
in bed the next day.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.490
An optimal fitness
program of the sort

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.220
that was covered in the optimal
fitness protocols episode

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.060
that I did is therefore one
that checks off the major boxes

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.760
that science tells us are
important for health span

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.470
and for lifespan,
and that can also

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.200
help us improve various
aspects of performance,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.560
and improve various
aspects of aesthetics,

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.690
whether it's fat loss or
muscle growth, if we choose.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.350
So without going into that
program in a lot of detail,

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.660
the core elements
of it are that it

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:59.050
includes at least 150
minutes and ideally

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:02.567
more like 200 minutes per week
of so-called zone 2 cardio.

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:04.150
I'll talk a little
bit more about zone

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:06.040
2 cardio a little bit
later in the episode.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.580
But zone 2 cardio is for,
those of you that don't know,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.530
a type of
cardiovascular exercise

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.480
that you can do
while maintaining

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.460
a conversation
without getting winded

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.130
but that if you were to
push a little bit harder,

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.890
that you would find it hard
to complete your sentences.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.470
In general, zone 2
cardio is the sort

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:25.957
that you can do while purely
nasal breathing, unless you

00:07:25.957 --> 00:07:27.790
need to talk, of course,
it's perfectly fine

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.350
to talk while doing
zone 2 cardio.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:30.730
And again the
scientific research

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:34.420
tells us that we should all be
getting at least 150 minutes

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.060
and probably more like 200
minutes of zone 2 cardio

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.420
per week.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:41.590
Now, in addition to that, a
foundational or optimal fitness

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.830
program for most people is
going to include anywhere

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.070
from 2-4 cardiovascular
training sessions that

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.830
are separate-- that's right--
separate from the zone 2

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.550
cardio, as well as 2-4
resistance or strength

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.430
training, sometimes also aimed
at hypertrophy or muscle growth

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:58.750
training sessions.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.930
Now, we have to acknowledge
that most people are probably

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.760
not going to hit the upper
threshold of all of those three

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:05.260
things.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.890
Most people simply do not have
the time and/or discipline

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:12.250
to get 200 minutes of zone
2 cardio per week, plus four

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.043
resistance training
sessions, plus four

00:08:15.043 --> 00:08:17.710
cardiovascular training sessions
that are separate from the zone

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:18.670
2 cardio.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.190
As a consequence, the
optimal fitness program

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.710
that I described in that
episode, and by the way,

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:25.330
it's the program
that I've essentially

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.970
followed for the last
30 years or more,

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:31.250
includes three cardiovascular
training sessions.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.450
So one of longer duration
sort of typical endurance type

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:35.950
training.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.380
This would be a long, slow
jog or a long hike one

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:41.710
day per week, as well as a
shorter cardiovascular training

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:45.628
session of about 25 to 30
minutes moving a bit faster,

00:08:45.628 --> 00:08:47.170
getting the heart
rate up a bit more,

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.030
breathing a little
bit harder, as well

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.187
as one very short cardiovascular
training session that

00:08:52.187 --> 00:08:54.520
would fall under the category
of high-intensity interval

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.940
training, things like sprints,
which don't necessarily

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:57.940
have to be done running.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:58.982
Could be done on a rower.

00:08:58.982 --> 00:09:01.220
It could be done on
a bike, et cetera.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:04.180
So three cardiovascular
training sessions, as well as

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.730
three resistance
training sessions.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.228
I want to acknowledge that
resistance training can

00:09:09.228 --> 00:09:10.270
be done with body weight.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.100
It can be done with
weights, with machines.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:13.780
I talked about the
differing virtues

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.360
of one approach versus
another, but nonetheless,

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.580
three resistance
training sessions.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:22.060
One focusing specifically
on leg training.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.143
One focused specifically
on torso training.

00:09:25.143 --> 00:09:27.310
That's right, chest,
shoulders, and back altogether,

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.500
as well as some neck training
for important reasons

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.100
that were mentioned
in that episode.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.280
And then a third
session that was

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.300
aimed at somewhat smaller body
parts, biceps, triceps, calves,

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:40.180
and some other small body
parts that tend to be neglected

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:44.170
and that are important to
train if one wants to encourage

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.900
muscular balance both
aesthetically and structurally

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:49.420
to avoid injury and
have excellent posture.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.930
So those were the core elements
of that foundational fitness

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:53.488
program.

00:09:53.488 --> 00:09:55.030
And I mentioned then
and I'll mention

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.210
again now that that
program is not a mandate.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.730
It is not an absolute
requirement for anyone

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:01.510
to follow.

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:04.090
It was simply meant as a
template from which people

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.550
could evaluate their own
existing fitness program,

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:08.610
perhaps modify it
somewhat or a lot,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:11.370
or if you were interested in
trying that specific fitness

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.110
program, that you could do that.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.440
And we have provided a link
both to that episode in the show

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:18.720
note captions, but we've
also provided a link

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.880
to a table or chart
that describes

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.710
that foundational
fitness program.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.990
It provides examples of
different cardiovascular

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.690
training and resistance
type training sessions.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:31.170
And it describes some potential
exercises and the rationale

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.240
for those exercises,
and the rationale

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.490
for selecting particular
repetition ranges

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:36.600
and rest between sets.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.277
All of that information is
available completely zero cost.

00:10:39.277 --> 00:10:41.110
You don't even need to
sign up for anything.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:43.770
You simply go to the link that
we provided in the show note

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:46.470
captions, and there's a
downloadable PDF there

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:47.640
for you to explore.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.500
Now, in addition
to the solo episode

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.990
that I did about optimal
fitness protocols,

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.450
we did a six-episode
guest series

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.630
with Dr. Andy Galpin, who
is a professor of physiology

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.030
at Cal State Fullerton
and a world expert

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:03.330
in all aspects of exercise
and muscle physiology.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.610
That episode described
a lot of the science,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.230
and in particular
science-backed tools

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.320
for improving everything
from long-distance endurance

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:16.180
to anaerobic capacity, strength,
hypertrophy, speed, power,

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:19.660
recovery, nutrition,
supplementation, and ways

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.390
to develop a year-long
program that will

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.250
ensure you ongoing progress.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.440
In that series, Dr.
Andy Galpin provided

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:30.580
an enormous amount of valuable
information, such that anyone

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.770
and everyone, meaning
the person who's

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:35.140
just interested in
starting a fitness program,

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.630
or improving their
existing fitness program,

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:41.260
or the elite athlete who
is interested in improving

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.910
their sprint times,
or their jump

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.000
height, or their powerlifting,
or their marathon time,

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.120
could clearly benefit from
some or all of the protocols

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:50.380
that he described.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:53.530
Now, because that series is so
extensive in terms of its depth

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.670
and breadth, again, providing
so much value at 0 cost thanks

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.520
to Dr. Andy Galpin's
expertise, but at the same time

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:03.280
because it might be a little
bit intimidating for many people

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.190
out there to try and figure out
which protocols to incorporate

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:08.050
into their existing
fitness regimen,

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:11.170
I thought it would be fun
and very beneficial to talk

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.300
about some of the key
tools that were described

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:15.190
throughout that
series that one could

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.770
consider incorporating into
their existing fitness routine

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:18.610
now.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:21.130
So that's what this
episode is really about.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.200
It's about the tools
that I personally

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.360
gleaned from those
discussions and that I

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:28.330
found to be of tremendous
value in improving

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.790
both my cardiovascular
fitness, my strength

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:33.220
and hypertrophy
training, my recovery,

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.500
and other aspects of my
overall fitness protocols.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:36.940
And when I say
beneficial, I mean

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.790
in terms of improving my
cardiovascular fitness,

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:42.250
improving my strength
and hypertrophy training,

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.440
and that have improved
the various metrics

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.290
of fitness lifespan
and health span which

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.080
include things like heart rate
variability, resting heart

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:53.950
rate, blood pressure, VO2 max,
as well as some of the fitness

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.710
metrics that were described
during that episode series

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:59.200
with Dr. Andy Galpin,
such as performance

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.810
metrics, the ability to
jump a certain distance,

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.180
the ability to run
a certain speed

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.100
or to run a certain
distance at a given speed,

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.590
the ability to move
weights in good form

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.090
for a certain number
of repetitions.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.460
Again, all of the metrics
of performance and health

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.890
are going to vary tremendously
from person to person

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.780
depending on where
you're starting,

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.090
how long you've been
training, and other aspects

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:21.130
of your health.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.050
The tools described
in today's episode

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:24.467
are designed for everybody.

00:13:24.467 --> 00:13:26.050
Again, these are
simple tools that you

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.540
can put into your existing
routine that should really

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.580
move the needle forward
in terms of improving

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.890
your overall levels
of fitness and health.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:35.890
OK, let's talk about the
tools to improve your fitness.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:39.220
The first tool is to
mesh your zone 2 cardio

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.330
with your daily activities.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:43.380
So for those of you
that don't know,

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:46.030
zone 2 cardio is
the type of movement

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.510
that we typically call
cardio exercise that

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:51.190
elevates your heart
rate somewhat,

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.410
increases your
breathing somewhat,

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.510
but that still allows
you to carry out

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:59.170
a conversation without having
to pause or to gasp in order

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:00.980
to complete your sentences.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:01.480
OK.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:04.570
So that's a general rule
of thumb for zone 2 cardio.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:07.420
Now, for those of you that
use a fitness tracker,

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:10.600
you can monitor whether or not
you are in zone 2 cardio very

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:11.560
precisely.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.380
But if you're like me and you
don't use a fitness tracker,

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:16.870
it's very easy to know if
you're in zone 2 cardio

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.570
because again, it's that
level of output that puts you

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:22.960
right below or somewhat
below the threshold where

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:26.230
if you were to exert yourself
with any more intensity,

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:29.410
that you wouldn't be able
to complete your sentences.

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.360
Now, this could, of
course, be evaluated

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.390
by jogging with someone or
walking with someone or hiking

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:36.850
with someone and carrying
out a conversation.

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.230
If somebody isn't available,
you could, of course,

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.087
do this by trying
to speak out loud

00:14:41.087 --> 00:14:42.670
and have a conversation
with yourself,

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.208
or if you want another way
to monitor whether or not

00:14:45.208 --> 00:14:47.500
you're in zone 2 cardio
without having to use a fitness

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:50.380
tracker, you could simply
ask yourself whether or not

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:53.380
you are maintaining a level
of output that increases

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:54.880
your heart rate
and your breathing

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:58.120
but that allows you to
maintain purely nasal breathing

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.620
the entire time.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.590
Any of those approaches
will tell you more or less

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.630
whether or not you're
in zone 2 cardio.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.530
Now, the scientific data
tell us that we should all

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:11.970
be getting anywhere from 150
minutes to 200 minutes per week

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.080
minimum of zone 2 cardio for
sake of cardiovascular health,

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.960
cerebrovascular health, and
a number of other aspects

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:21.810
of health that are important
essentially to everybody

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.110
for health span and life span

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.080
Now, many people
including myself, schedule

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:30.350
zone 2 cardio into their
weekly fitness regimen.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.450
So for me, I have
one day a week.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:34.790
For me it falls
on a Sunday where

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:37.840
I go out for a jog that lasts
anywhere from 60 minutes

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:38.820
to 90 minutes.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:39.980
It's a slow jog.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:42.022
I can maintain nasal
breathing the entire time

00:15:42.022 --> 00:15:43.730
or have a conversation
with somebody else

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:47.090
or myself the entire
time if I like.

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:50.360
Or sometimes it
consists of a hike

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.990
by myself or with other people,
and sometimes those hikes

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.210
extend anywhere from
an hour to four hours

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:58.730
depending on the
circumstances, et cetera.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:00.680
I will mention that
whenever possible, I try

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.920
and do that once a week zone
2 cardio session out of doors

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:06.420
because I like being in nature,
and I like getting sunlight,

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.280
and I like getting fresh air.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.000
Now, during the discussion
with Dr. Andy Galpin,

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.580
I explained how I
get my zone 2 cardio.

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:16.850
And I acknowledged that
once-a-week session doesn't

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.290
always allow me to
reach that 150-minute

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:23.950
to 200-minute minimum threshold
of zone 2 cardio per week.

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:26.100
Sometimes it does,
sometimes it doesn't.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.470
And his response to that
was very reassuring.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:32.730
What he said was, look, if you
want to schedule zone 2 cardio

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:36.760
and head out for a long
Sunday jog or hike, terrific.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.680
If you want to schedule a zone
2 cardio as two or more sessions

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:43.680
on the treadmill or
on the bike, great.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.320
But that he doesn't
actually think of zone 2

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:48.840
cardio as exercise at all.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.690
And to that I gasped, and then
I was a little bit deflated.

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:52.710
I thought, oh, great.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.780
I've been doing all
this zone 2 cardio,

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:56.760
and you don't even
consider that exercise.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:58.862
And then what he said
was very reassuring,

00:16:58.862 --> 00:17:01.320
and I think it's going to be
very reassuring to all of you.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:04.440
He said, first of
all, zone 2 cardio

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.619
is absolutely critical to our
health for a number of reasons

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:09.030
that I already mentioned.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:13.349
But in addition to that, zone
2 cardio does not impede,

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:17.040
and in fact, can enhance our
other aspects of fitness.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.630
So for example, our
strength training,

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.150
our hypertrophy training,
or any type of speed work,

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.910
or other types of cardiovascular
training one might do.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:26.040
And that the best
way to get zone

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.180
2 cardio is OK, if you want
to schedule it, schedule it

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.110
as a session.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:34.590
But that to simply increase
the amount of walking

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:38.130
and in particular, walking at
a rapid pace that one does,

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:40.260
and to increase the
total amount of movement

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:41.885
that one's getting
throughout the week.

00:17:41.885 --> 00:17:44.640
So taking groceries in and
out of the grocery store,

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.880
running around with the kids,
taking a walk with a coworker

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:50.640
while having a work discussion,
taking your calls for work

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.700
while pacing in the
office or going outside,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.190
what he impressed on me
is that zone 2 cardio

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:58.410
can be meshed throughout
the daily activities

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:01.140
that I and everybody else
generally have to do.

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:04.560
And this was of great relief
to me because I as many of you

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:06.990
are, I'm extremely busy.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.952
I don't have time to schedule
in more cardio per week,

00:18:09.952 --> 00:18:11.910
or at least I don't see
the way I could do that

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:14.610
without reducing the amount
of sleep that I'm getting,

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:16.998
or without reducing the
amount of social connection

00:18:16.998 --> 00:18:19.290
that I'm getting with family
and friends, both of which

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.810
are extremely important to
our mental health and physical

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:22.450
health.

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:25.320
So the basic tool
here is yes, get

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:28.530
200 minutes per week
minimum of zone 2 cardio.

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:31.788
And notice I said 200 minutes,
not 150 minutes to 200 minutes.

00:18:31.788 --> 00:18:33.330
I'm going to set
the higher threshold

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:37.170
of 200 minutes per week
minimum of zone 2 cardio.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.822
But that you don't need
to schedule that as time

00:18:39.822 --> 00:18:40.530
on the treadmill.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:42.040
If you want to, great.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.150
But what was communicated to
me from Dr. Andy Galpin is

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.318
that zone 2 cardio is
immensely beneficial,

00:18:47.318 --> 00:18:49.110
it's not going to
impede, and in fact, it's

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.900
going to improve other
aspects of fitness,

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:54.780
and that it does not have
to impede and it in fact

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.450
can improve other
aspects of our daily life

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:00.300
like our ability to engage
socially, our ability

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:03.420
to have a great output at
work in whatever type of work

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:04.300
you do.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.470
So the message is very simple,
get 200 minutes or more

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:08.910
of zone 2 cardio per week.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.490
And the message is also
a very reassuring one

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:14.310
which is that zone 2
cardio can be spread

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:15.810
throughout your
daily activities,

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:17.910
and that if you're
doing enough of it,

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.520
you probably don't even have to
count the total amount of zone

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:21.810
2 cardio that you're getting.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.060
If you simply make the
effort to move around

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.640
a lot more during
your daily activities

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:30.032
and to mesh that zone 2 cardio
with your daily activities,

00:19:30.032 --> 00:19:31.740
you're going to hit
that threshold of 200

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:33.360
minutes per week minimum.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.220
Now, that's a great
message for me

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.100
because I'm already doing
the three resistance training

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:38.910
workouts per week.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.800
I'm doing what now
I can just call

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:43.620
the two other cardiovascular
training workouts per week

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:46.710
because now I don't even count
that long Sunday jog or Sunday

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:47.940
hike as exercise.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:51.510
I just consider that movement
out of doors on the weekend.

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:54.180
And in doing so, it's also
allowed me to really enjoy

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.110
that a lot more.

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:57.120
There's something about
considering something

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:01.170
a fitness training program that
shifts it from just recreation

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:03.180
and enjoying life to training.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.513
And I, of course, love training.

00:20:04.513 --> 00:20:07.055
I love training in the gym, and
I love training out of doors.

00:20:07.055 --> 00:20:07.712
I love running.

00:20:07.712 --> 00:20:08.670
I love lifting weights.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.530
I love all sorts of
physical training.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:13.080
I know many people do, I
know many people don't.

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.960
But if one looks
at zone 2 cardio

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.940
as just part of
their daily life,

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:21.928
you're far more likely to get
that zone 2 cardio in and all

00:20:21.928 --> 00:20:23.220
the benefits that come with it.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:27.900
And you're also opening up time
for work for social engagements

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:31.185
and to do and pay attention
to other aspects of fitness,

00:20:31.185 --> 00:20:33.060
which is what we're
going to talk about next.

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:35.430
The second tool that I've
incorporated into my fitness

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.730
regimen and that I believe
can be of great benefit

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:44.040
to frankly everybody is to
start including low repetition,

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:46.360
pure strength work.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:49.350
Now, some of you may already
be doing low repetition,

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:50.770
pure strength work.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.640
But I believe that
most people don't.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:55.440
Most people who do
resistance training

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:57.360
are using either
machines, or free weights,

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:59.940
or some combination of
those, or perhaps they're

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.020
using body weight.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:06.690
And they tend to focus on
repetition ranges from about 5

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.810
and usually more like
6 repetitions out

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:13.120
to about 10 and
perhaps 15 repetitions.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.780
Now, of course, doing resistance
training and repetition

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:19.740
ranges of 5-15 reps
per set, provided

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:21.840
it's done at
sufficient intensity,

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:24.860
so either to failure or
close to failure, of course,

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:27.235
in good form is
tremendously beneficial.

00:21:27.235 --> 00:21:28.360
It can help build strength.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:30.100
It can enhance hypertrophy.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.710
There is tremendous value to
training in those repetition

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:33.550
ranges.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.950
But when I sat down
with Dr. Andy Galpin

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:38.710
to discuss resistance
training, specifically, he

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:42.100
made it very clear that
at least for some portion

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:44.540
of one's yearly training cycle.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:48.490
So perhaps eight weeks or
10 weeks, or in the case

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:52.630
that I adopted 12 weeks,
there is tremendous benefit

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:56.560
to training in the 3 to 5
repetition range and maybe even

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:57.290
lower.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.620
So the second tool of
training specifically

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:01.780
for strength in the 3
to 5 repetition range

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.810
is something that I started to
incorporate after I sat down

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:06.070
to record that series.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:08.230
And I'll just tell you
a few of the benefits

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:09.730
that I've experienced,
and then I'll

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:11.350
tell you the specific
protocol that

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.040
makes it very easy to do this.

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:16.420
The most obvious benefit to me
was that I got much stronger.

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:18.640
And that that strength
persisted such that

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.400
when I went back to using
higher repetition ranges,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.500
so typically I train
with weights or machines

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:26.440
in the 6 to 10 repetition range,
sometimes a little higher,

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:27.610
sometimes a little lower.

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:30.370
But never before
had I specifically

00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:32.380
trained in the 3 to
5 repetition range

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:35.740
exclusively for a period
of 10 to 12 weeks.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.980
And when I did that, I, of
course, gained strength.

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:40.660
But that strength
stayed with me such

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:42.730
that when I returned to
higher repetition ranges,

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.520
I could use more weight in
good form, and that, of course,

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:48.070
enhanced strength and
hypertrophy further.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:51.640
In addition, there was another
effect that was at least to me

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:55.240
very unexpected, which was
that my cardiovascular training

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:56.712
improved significantly.

00:22:56.712 --> 00:22:57.670
Now, why would this be?

00:22:57.670 --> 00:22:59.980
Because typically a
3 to 5 repetition set

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.965
does not elevate the
heart rate for long enough

00:23:01.965 --> 00:23:04.090
that you would consider it
cardiovascular training.

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.173
And of course, the rest
periods between those sets

00:23:06.173 --> 00:23:07.450
is pretty long as well.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.700
So even if heart rate goes
up during those heavy sets,

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:12.580
it's going to go down during
those long 3 to 5 minute rest

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:14.170
periods between those sets.

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:18.670
But what I noticed was that my
overall posture and my ability

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:20.590
to maintain
cardiovascular output

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:23.980
while using good running
form or good rowing form

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.900
was also vastly improved.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.630
And the logical interpretation
of why that would be

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:31.960
is simply that the
muscles got stronger,

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:34.060
and those same muscles
are being incorporated

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:37.750
into the cardiovascular--
let's call it endurance work

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:39.100
that I'm doing on other days.

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:42.160
And therefore, I can carry out
those cardiovascular training

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.280
sessions in better form
for longer periods of time.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:48.070
I actually felt much stronger
during my cardiovascular

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:51.220
training as I got much stronger
moving these heavier weight

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:53.410
loads for low repetition sets.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.200
And then the third specific
benefit that I noticed

00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:01.480
is that when training heavy
for 3 to 5 repetitions per set,

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:03.160
I didn't get sore.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:05.590
And this to me was
an incredible benefit

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.990
because typically when I
train in the 6 repetition

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:10.090
to 15 repetition
range and I take

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:12.340
those sets to failure
or near failure,

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:15.070
I do experience some
soreness the next day.

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:17.560
Ordinarily that soreness
isn't so intense

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.000
that it prevents
me from doing any

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:20.837
of the other sorts of
workouts that I do.

00:24:20.837 --> 00:24:22.420
And for those of you
that have visited

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:24.070
that foundational
fitness protocol,

00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:27.550
you know that I hit each major
and minor muscle group once

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:31.570
per week directly as well
as once per week indirectly.

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:34.000
That's the overall
structure of that program

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.300
in order to allow
sufficient recovery

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:38.050
between those resistance
training workouts

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.150
to be able to make
continual progress.

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:43.330
Now, by training in this
3 to 5 repetition range

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:45.010
that Dr. Andy
Galpin suggested, I

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:46.720
was able to improve
my strength, improve

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:49.390
my cardiovascular
output, reduce soreness.

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.170
I also just felt better overall.

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:53.830
I had a lot more energy after
those workouts than I typically

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:56.350
do after my resistance
training sessions

00:24:56.350 --> 00:24:58.098
when I use higher
repetition ranges.

00:24:58.098 --> 00:24:59.890
There are just a number
of different things

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.890
that made me feel, wow, this
is really a powerful protocol.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.890
And of course, moving
heavier weights in the gym

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:06.950
feels good too.

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:08.200
It feels good to get stronger.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.492
At least there's a positive
feedback loop there for me,

00:25:10.492 --> 00:25:11.980
and I think for most people.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.290
And I should also mention
that for those of you that

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:16.870
are averse to doing heavier
resistance training in the 3

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:19.690
to 5 repetition range because
you fear that it will make you

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:23.420
too big or too bulky, training
in the low repetition ranges

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:26.390
is actually more geared
towards increasing strength

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:30.260
and is shifting away somewhat
from increasing hypertrophy

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:31.850
or muscle size.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:34.250
So that's a great
benefit for those of you

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:36.110
that want to be
strong and also want

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:37.820
to maintain
cardiovascular fitness,

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:39.860
but you don't want
to add muscular size.

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:42.865
And of course, for all of you
that want to add muscular size,

00:25:42.865 --> 00:25:44.990
it's well established that
increasing your strength

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:46.730
will allow you then
to return to patterns

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:48.105
of hypertrophy
training that will

00:25:48.105 --> 00:25:49.580
allow you to use
heavier weights,

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:52.190
and therefore induce
greater hypertrophy.

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:55.490
So there are oh, so many reasons
to incorporate these strength

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:56.700
training protocols.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.910
So the way that Dr.
Andy Galpin suggested

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:01.010
one do it and was the
way that I did it is he

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:03.260
used this 3 by 5 protocol.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:06.120
The 3 by 5 protocol is
very straightforward.

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:10.680
It involves doing three to
five exercises per workout.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:11.180
OK.

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.820
So if it's a workout for legs,
it's three to five exercises.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:17.450
If it's a workout for
some upper body muscle,

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:19.010
it's three to five exercises.

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:25.430
Three to five exercises for
3 to 5 sets per exercise,

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:30.620
3 to 5 repetitions per
set, and 3 to 5 minutes

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:33.690
of rest between each set.

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:36.120
In addition, he
emphasized that one

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:39.300
can do those workouts three
to five times per week.

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.310
Although, I'm going
to put an asterisk

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:44.280
next to that last statement
because I found that I couldn't

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:47.670
do the 3 by 5
protocol say for legs

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:50.038
specifically three to
five times per week.

00:26:50.038 --> 00:26:52.080
I realize that might be
possible for some people,

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:55.110
but I'm somebody who like
many of you out there,

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:58.860
either doesn't have the time
or doesn't have the recovery

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:02.880
capacity to train my legs
three to five times per week.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:05.190
Even though I acknowledge
that there are probably

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.620
ways to do that would
still allow me to recover,

00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:11.310
it just simply starts to impede
into other areas of training.

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:15.120
It starts to impede other areas
of life like work and family

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:17.290
and sleep and all the rest.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:20.490
So what I did and what
I'm suggesting you try

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:24.720
is for any existing resistance
training that you're doing,

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:29.340
to take a period of 8 or
10 or ideally 12 weeks

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:33.490
and do the vast majority, if not
all of that resistance training

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.940
in the lower repetition range
that's designed specifically

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.070
to induce strength adaptations.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:40.960
And to not pay attention
to whether or not

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.600
you're hitting that same muscle
group three to five times

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:46.630
per week, rather if you
train your legs once or twice

00:27:46.630 --> 00:27:49.420
per week, to simply do all
of the work for your legs

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:51.340
in that 3 to 5 repetition range.

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:54.070
If you train an upper body
muscle or muscle groups,

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:57.700
chest, shoulders, back
once per week or twice

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:00.100
per week to just stay
within that 3 to 5

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:03.460
repetition range
for those work sets.

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:05.230
Warm-ups can include
a few more reps.

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:08.290
And then to adhere
to this three to five

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:11.650
exercises, 3 to 5
sets per exercise,

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:16.330
3 to 5 repetitions per set, and
3 to 5 minutes between sets.

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:19.540
Now, the one exception to
this that I incorporated

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:22.100
was that for very
small muscle groups.

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:25.720
So for instance, the rear
deltoids or for neck work,

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:31.480
or for calf work to not rely
purely on 3 to 5 repetitions

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:33.640
but maybe to work in
a range of anywhere

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:35.810
from 5 to 8 repetitions.

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:39.460
So still fairly low
repetitions but not so low

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:41.650
that it restricts you
to 3 to 5 repetitions.

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:44.860
The reason for that is that I
and I think a lot of people out

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:47.770
there find it hard to
fatigue those smaller muscle

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:51.220
groups adequately with good
form when restricting oneself

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:52.690
to those low repetitions.

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:54.490
However, for big
compound movements

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:57.850
like presses and squats and
deadlifts and glute ham raises

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:01.150
and things of that sort, maybe
even leg extensions and leg

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:04.960
curls, which are isolation
exercises, of course, to really

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:08.140
restrict oneself to those
3 to 5 repetition ranges

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:10.720
that take you to
failure or near failure.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:13.720
I listed off the benefits of
doing that that I experienced,

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:15.520
and I'm confident
that you will also

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:17.240
experience a lot of benefits.

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:20.170
So just to remind you what
some of those benefits are,

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:22.540
you get stronger,
which feels great.

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:24.520
That occurs within
your weight workouts,

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:28.660
but it also carries over to your
endurance training sessions.

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:31.150
I also noticed
that when returning

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:33.620
to higher repetitions
for resistance training,

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:37.690
so after 12 weeks shifting away
from 3 to 5 repetition ranges

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:40.870
and going back to training
in the 6 to 10 repetition

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:42.550
ranges mainly.

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:45.490
Occasionally up to 12 or 15
but really mainly restricting

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:50.080
to 6 to 10 repetitions that you
can move much heavier weights

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:52.690
in good form, and thereby
induce more hypertrophy

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:55.900
while still also continuing
to gain some strength.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:58.790
And another benefit was
again reduced soreness

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.790
compared to when training
with higher repetition

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:03.790
ranges and more
mental freshness,

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:05.290
I guess the only
way to describe it,

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:08.080
when training in those
lower repetition ranges.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:09.520
I don't know about
you, but when I

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:12.760
finish a really hard
hour-long resistance training

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:16.000
session done in the 6
to 12 repetition range,

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:17.920
there's a certain
type of mental fatigue

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:19.960
that even if I eat
properly afterwards,

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:22.030
even if I hydrate
properly, that it

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:24.790
tends to sap a bit of my
mental energy later in the day.

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:27.580
But that the training at
the 3 to 5 repetition range

00:30:27.580 --> 00:30:29.000
did just the opposite.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:31.750
It actually enhanced my
focus and my cognition,

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:33.760
my overall levels
of physical energy,

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:35.650
which is great
because it allows you

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:37.660
to do all the other
things that we're required

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:38.720
to do throughout the day.

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:40.420
And by the way,
it will also allow

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:42.490
you to get more of
that zone 2 cardio.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:45.520
So if you want more details
on the 3 by 5 protocol,

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:48.880
again, that's time stamped
in the relevant episode

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:51.820
on strength and hypertrophy that
we did with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:54.850
I'll also provide a link to that
specific time stamp in the show

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:56.590
note caption to this episode.

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:59.440
I do want to point out that
you don't just jump right

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:01.330
into heavy sets of
3 to 5 repetitions,

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:03.070
you need to warm up adequately.

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:06.460
For some people that warm-up
will be higher repetition sets,

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:09.400
so say 10 to 12 repetitions
with just the empty bar

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:10.150
or a lightweight.

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:11.733
And then adding a
little bit of weight

00:31:11.733 --> 00:31:14.260
and doing 8 repetitions
and maybe 6 repetitions,

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:15.790
and then your work
sets as they're

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:17.650
called of 3 to 5 repetitions.

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:20.650
Or perhaps you're
like me and you

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:22.660
prefer to do low
repetition warm-ups.

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:25.060
So this was also something
that I discussed with Dr. Andy

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:28.930
Galpin and that for me has made
a tremendous positive impact

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:31.480
on all my resistance training,
regardless of whether or not

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.650
it is low repetition
or high repetition.

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:37.280
And that's to do a brief
warm set that is somewhere

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:38.990
in the range of 6
to 8 repetitions,

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:41.390
very light just to get
familiar with the movement.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:43.430
Then to do a second
warm-up set that

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:47.180
includes some load on the bar or
the free weight or the machine.

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:49.355
And then a second warm-up set--

00:31:49.355 --> 00:31:51.230
again, this could be
free weights or machines

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:53.330
that incorporates
a bit more load

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:57.500
but still keeps the repetitions
low so in the 4 to 6

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:58.310
repetition range.

00:31:58.310 --> 00:31:59.870
And then maybe,
especially if it's

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.420
at the beginning of the workout
and my core body temperature

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.970
isn't elevated yet,
I'll do a third warm-up.

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:07.340
But that third warm up
which, of course, is

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:09.680
going to be progressively
a little bit heavier

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:11.090
than the first or
second warm-up,

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:13.700
is still going to fall within
the low repetition range

00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:15.800
so just 2 to 4 repetitions.

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:18.380
For me, including a
few more warm-up sets

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:21.260
with progressively heavier
weight on each warm

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.410
up but still keeping the
total repetition count

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:28.310
low, so somewhere in the
range of 2 to 6 repetitions,

00:32:28.310 --> 00:32:31.910
has been very beneficial
for improving my work

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:34.933
output during the so-called
work sets regardless of

00:32:34.933 --> 00:32:37.350
whether or not I'm training
in the 3 to 5 repetition range

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:40.110
or whether or not I'm
training in the 6 to 15

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:40.885
repetition range.

00:32:40.885 --> 00:32:43.260
I know for some people, this
might be kind of surprising,

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:46.140
how is it that my work sets
are actually higher repetition

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:47.490
than my warm-up sets?

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.410
Or put differently,
how and why is it

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:52.230
that my warm-up sets are lower
repetition than my work sets?

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:54.930
And that's because I fall
into this category of people

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.810
that tends to fatigue
pretty quickly when

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:59.290
doing resistance training.

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:01.740
So for me, keeping
the repetition count

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.290
on any individual
warm up set pretty low

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.170
has allowed me to really
improve my strength output

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:09.330
and really improve my
strength and hypertrophy

00:33:09.330 --> 00:33:11.760
training when I shift to
the so-called work sets.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.252
So I already listed off a
number of important documented

00:33:14.252 --> 00:33:15.960
benefits and benefits
that I've certainly

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:19.740
experienced by incorporating
low repetition,

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:22.950
pure strength work into
my yearly training cycle

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:25.650
for periods of 8 to 12 weeks.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:29.010
In addition to that, during
my conversation with Dr. Andy

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:31.050
Galpin, he said
something very important

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:32.850
for everyone to
hear and understand.

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:35.730
He said, when you look at the
data on aging and performance,

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:37.830
in particular
muscular performance,

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:41.130
you see some very interesting
patterns within the data.

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:45.600
He said for instance, that
for every year after age 40,

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:48.870
there's a 1% drop in
muscle size that can be

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:50.310
offset by resistance training.

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:52.530
But that if you don't
do resistance training,

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:54.030
that you won't offset.

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.310
And during that
series, we also talked

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:59.220
about the minimum
requirement for 6

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:01.470
and probably more
like 10 working sets

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:05.580
per muscle group per week in
order to at least maintain

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:09.659
muscle size, not just age
40 and beyond but even

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:10.360
at younger ages.

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:10.860
OK.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:11.909
So that's muscle size.

00:34:11.909 --> 00:34:15.010
1% decrease per year unless
you do the right thing.

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:17.520
And the right thing is get 6
to 10 working sets per week

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:19.389
in order to offset
that decrease.

00:34:19.389 --> 00:34:21.389
And if you train
properly for hypertrophy,

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:24.900
yes, you can still increase
muscle size past age 40.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:28.409
In addition, he said
that there is a 3% to 5%

00:34:28.409 --> 00:34:34.469
reduction per year for every
year past age 40 in strength

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:35.670
and power.

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:37.980
Now, that's a very
important metric

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:41.940
because what it's telling us is
that the drop off in strength

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:45.270
and power is significantly
greater per each year

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:49.260
after age 40 than is the
decrease in muscle size,

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:51.330
telling us that we
have to do something

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:54.570
to offset that decrease
in strength and power.

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:58.200
In addition, he mentioned that
for every year past age 40,

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:05.070
there is an 8% to 10% decrease
in speed and in explosiveness.

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:08.520
And so if one is interested
in maintaining speed

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:10.410
of muscular movement
and explosiveness

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:12.450
of muscular movement,
something that's perhaps

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.670
important to a number
of you, one also

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.890
has to incorporate training
specifically geared

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:20.520
toward maintaining or improving
speed and explosiveness.

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:23.280
Now, I like many people am
not so interested in speed

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:25.720
and explosiveness, I know
they have their utility,

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.350
but I am interested in
maintaining muscle size

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:31.170
over the course of my life,
perhaps even adding some muscle

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:33.300
to a particular muscle groups.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:37.290
I'm also very interested in at
least maintaining and ideally

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:39.720
even gaining some strength
in certain muscle groups

00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:41.560
throughout my entire lifespan.

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:43.530
And that's not just for
performance reasons.

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:46.230
That's also because we know
that maintaining or improving

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.810
strength of our muscles
is very important

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:51.390
across the entire
lifespan but especially

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:54.750
in the years spanning
from 40 until death,

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:58.290
which I think for most people
falls somewhere between 50,

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:02.880
60, 70, or ideally, out into
the 80s and 90s or 100, right?

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.900
That's what we're all seeking is
to die later in better health.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:10.920
And in order to do that, we have
to dedicate some very specific

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:13.770
training protocols in order
to maintain or build strength.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:16.647
So to summarize, in addition to
all the positive reasons to do

00:36:16.647 --> 00:36:18.480
dedicated strength
training that I mentioned

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:22.020
before, it's highly recommended
that you do some dedicated

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:24.990
strength training for the
purposes of offsetting

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.750
the age-related decline in
strength that occurs again

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:31.470
3% to 5% per year
past age 40, which is

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:33.070
a pretty significant decline.

00:36:33.070 --> 00:36:35.230
But the good news is
if you do the 3 to 5

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:37.960
protocol for say
12 weeks per year,

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:40.000
and then you continue
to resistance train

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:43.360
using other repetition ranges
geared towards hypertrophy

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:45.820
and strength or perhaps
even muscular endurance,

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:48.010
the good news is you'll
maintain your strength

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:49.870
and perhaps even
build your strength,

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:53.380
offsetting that natural decrease
that would otherwise occur.

