00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:09.530
The next tool I'm
about to describe

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:11.510
relates to your
cardiovascular training,

00:38:11.510 --> 00:38:13.190
and it's a tool that
can greatly improve

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.760
your cardiovascular fitness
with a limited amount of time

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:17.540
commitment.

00:38:17.540 --> 00:38:20.190
But that is not
to say it is easy.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:23.330
What I'm referring to is
the so-called Sugarcane.

00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:25.730
If you listened to the
series with Dr. Andy Galpin,

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:28.590
you may recall our discussion
about the Sugarcane,

00:38:28.590 --> 00:38:32.300
which is so named after our
friend and expert trainer Kenny

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:33.020
Kane.

00:38:33.020 --> 00:38:36.620
And it is a very efficient
yet somewhat brutal

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:39.420
way to increase your
cardiovascular output.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:41.840
So the Sugarcane is
the type of protocol

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:44.610
that you would incorporate
once in the period of a week

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:45.890
but certainly not every week.

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:49.430
It's the kind of thing that you
might throw in once every two

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:52.370
weeks or once every four
weeks as a replacement

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:55.130
for your other high
intensity interval training.

00:38:55.130 --> 00:38:58.580
The Sugarcane involves
selecting some form of exercise

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:01.880
that you can do at
high intensity safely.

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:04.010
That, of course, will
differ between individuals.

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:06.170
For some of you it will
be a stationary bike.

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:08.420
For others of you, it
will be a road bike.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:10.040
For others of you,
it will be running.

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:12.200
And for others of you,
it will be rowing.

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:15.660
The exact form of
exercise is not important.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.690
What is important
is that you can

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:19.530
generate a lot of intensity.

00:39:19.530 --> 00:39:22.550
So you're going to be doing some
sprint like work, although not

00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:25.670
all out sprints except
on the final round.

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:27.900
I'll explain where all
this is going in a moment.

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:29.840
But again, you need
to select a movement

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:33.530
that you can do without injuring
yourself while still performing

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:34.980
a movement at high intensity.

00:39:34.980 --> 00:39:36.830
So for me that would
be running, for you

00:39:36.830 --> 00:39:38.340
it might be something else.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:41.210
The Sugarcane is pretty
straightforward in structure.

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:45.000
It involves three rounds after
a brief warm-up, of course.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:46.980
So you're going to do 3
to 5 minutes of jogging

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:48.410
or jumping jacks
or skipping rope,

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.202
something to get your
core body temperature

00:39:50.202 --> 00:39:53.430
up so that you're prepared to
do the high-intensity work.

00:39:53.430 --> 00:39:57.410
And then there are only three
rounds of high-intensity work.

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:01.400
And they go as the
following, in round 1,

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.410
you're going to take
2 minutes-- so you'll

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:05.150
need to set a timer
for 2 minutes--

00:40:05.150 --> 00:40:08.000
and you're going to go the
maximum distance that you

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:09.800
can in that 2 minutes.

00:40:09.800 --> 00:40:13.250
So run the maximum distance that
you can for 2 minutes, or cycle

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:15.440
the maximum distance that
you can for 2 minutes,

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.360
or VersaClimber the
maximum distance

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:18.950
that you can for 2 minutes.

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:20.720
Whatever you
select, you're going

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:23.660
to do that as far and
as fast as you can

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:26.210
for the duration of 2 minutes.

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:28.220
So depending on the
movement and depending

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:30.140
on your level of
fitness, that distance

00:40:30.140 --> 00:40:34.700
might be 400m, 600m,
800m, et cetera.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:37.210
Whatever distance you
travel in that 2 minutes,

00:40:37.210 --> 00:40:40.510
you are going to mark that
distance down in your mind,

00:40:40.510 --> 00:40:42.520
or in your phone, or
on a piece of paper.

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:46.150
And then you're going
to rest 2 minutes.

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:48.760
So 2 minutes of work
then rest 2 minutes.

00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:53.560
Then in round 2, you're going
to go the same distance that you

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:57.760
did in round 1, and you're
going to take as much time

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:01.670
as you need to do that
distance as fast as you can.

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:07.480
So if you went 600m in 2
minutes for round 1, in round 2

00:41:07.480 --> 00:41:10.450
you're going to
go 600m, and it's

00:41:10.450 --> 00:41:12.850
going to take you however
long it takes you.

00:41:12.850 --> 00:41:17.530
Chances are if you really did
the best you could in round 1,

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:20.110
you were at maximum output
for the first 2 minutes,

00:41:20.110 --> 00:41:22.180
that in round 2 it's
going to take you

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:25.250
longer than 2 minutes to travel
that equivalent distance.

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:27.010
However, there is
the possibility

00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:28.540
that it will take you less time.

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:31.370
But for most people, it's
going to take you more time.

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:35.060
So staying with this example of
600m in 2 minutes on round 1,

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:37.970
in round 2, you're
going to go 600m,

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:41.780
let's say it takes
you 2 minutes and 30s.

00:41:41.780 --> 00:41:45.860
You then are going to mark
down how long round 2 took you.

00:41:45.860 --> 00:41:50.240
So in this case, the example
is 2 minutes and 30s.

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:53.000
Then you're going to
rest another 2 minutes.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:55.640
And then in round
3, you're going

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:59.750
to go all out again
as fast and as safely

00:41:59.750 --> 00:42:03.860
as you can for the same duration
that you did in round 2.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:09.300
And your goal is to go at least
as far as you went in round 1.

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:11.480
And if there's still
time left, you're

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:15.590
going to continue to go all out
again as fast as you safely can

00:42:15.590 --> 00:42:18.570
until the entire
duration is completed.

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:21.560
So it's really just three
rounds with two rest periods

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.660
in between round 1 and round 3.

00:42:23.660 --> 00:42:26.570
And then I highly recommend
that after round 3,

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:28.850
that you do some sort
of dedicated cooldown.

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:31.220
So instead of just flopping
on to the bench or the floor

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:34.080
or the lawn, that you walk
around slowly until you

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:35.250
recover your breathing.

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:37.320
The reason I like the
sugarcane as a tool

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:40.080
that one implements once
every say two to four weeks

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:43.260
as a replacement for one's
typical high-intensity interval

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:44.860
training is several fold.

00:42:44.860 --> 00:42:46.980
First of all, if you
provide the right intensity

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:50.490
in round 1 and
round 2 and round 3,

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:53.100
it is sure to elevate your
heart rate substantially,

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:55.620
and in doing so,
improve your VO2 max,

00:42:55.620 --> 00:42:58.260
which is correlated with all
sorts of important metrics

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:01.020
related to health span,
performance, and life span.

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:03.820
Second of all, it gamifies
things a little bit.

00:43:03.820 --> 00:43:06.240
It pits you against
yourself in the sense

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:09.510
that if you go out at
maximum speed, again,

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:11.790
performing a movement
that you can safely

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:15.030
perform at maximum speed
in round 1, well, then

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:18.770
you have something to compete
against in round 2 and round 3.

00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:20.520
And that makes the
high-intensity interval

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:23.070
training, first of
all, very intense,

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:26.160
but also it makes
it fun in a way that

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:29.040
lets you forget just how
painful the whole thing is.

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:31.260
The next tool to
improve your fitness

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:34.590
is called exercise
snacks and as the name

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:38.340
suggests this is a fun one
and was suggested by Dr. Andy

00:43:38.340 --> 00:43:41.760
Galpin as a way to either
enhance or maintain

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.670
your fitness depending on
how your core or foundational

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:46.360
fitness program is going.

00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:48.240
So when I say core I
don't mean your abs.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.730
I mean whether or not you're
getting your regular cardio

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:52.470
and your regular
resistance training

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:55.410
if you were to add one or
several of these exercise

00:43:55.410 --> 00:43:56.680
snacks per week.

00:43:56.680 --> 00:43:59.220
It can further improve
things like VO2 max,

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:00.840
muscular endurance, et cetera.

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:02.670
I'll talk about
the specific snacks

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:04.930
that you will be
doing in a moment.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:07.480
However, as Dr. Andy
Galpin also pointed out,

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:09.810
there are times in
which we happen to not

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:12.630
be following our foundational
fitness program either

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:16.710
because work demands or family
demands or we're traveling.

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:19.710
We're simply not keeping
up with our basic routine.

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:21.810
And under those
conditions, exercise snacks

00:44:21.810 --> 00:44:24.660
are a terrific way to maintain
the fitness that you've already

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:28.770
built and developed, and you
don't lose any ground in a week

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:30.720
say where you get
particularly busy.

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.960
Now, exercise snacks can take
on a variety of different forms.

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:36.540
But for sake of
simplicity and clarity,

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:39.460
today we're going to divide
them into two major categories.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:41.880
The first category
are exercise snacks

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:44.190
that are going to
improve or maintain

00:44:44.190 --> 00:44:47.130
your cardiovascular fitness,
so your ability to run

00:44:47.130 --> 00:44:52.410
or cycle or row some distance
say 12 minutes or longer.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:55.050
But keep in mind these exercise
snacks are very, very brief.

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:57.940
They don't require that you do
them for 12 minutes or longer.

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:00.900
What they are going to do is
either maintain or enhance

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:03.270
the type of
endurance that allows

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:06.510
you to continue in an activity
for 12 minutes or longer.

00:45:06.510 --> 00:45:08.550
The second category
of exercise snack

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:10.720
relates to muscular endurance.

00:45:10.720 --> 00:45:13.590
Muscular endurance is a very
important aspect of fitness.

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:15.270
And even though some
people are already

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:17.010
training for muscular
endurance, it's

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:20.520
something that most people are
not doing enough training for.

00:45:20.520 --> 00:45:22.110
Muscular endurance
is your ability

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:25.680
say to maintain a wall set
or to maintain a plank,

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:27.690
or to do the maximum
number of push-ups

00:45:27.690 --> 00:45:29.323
that you can do in one session.

00:45:29.323 --> 00:45:31.740
The sort of drop to the floor
and give me as many push-ups

00:45:31.740 --> 00:45:33.990
as you possibly can type of
thing, or as many sit-ups

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:35.430
as you can type of thing.

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:37.290
Muscular endurance
translates to a number

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:39.300
of other aspects of
fitness, and it's something

00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:41.110
that we should
all be working on.

00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:43.590
And again, many people just
don't make space for it

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:44.980
in their regular routine.

00:45:44.980 --> 00:45:47.790
So now we have these two
categories of exercise snacks.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:51.030
One geared toward
enhancing or maintaining

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:54.090
your cardiovascular
fitness as it translates

00:45:54.090 --> 00:45:57.810
to longer duration endurance
activities, so 12 minutes

00:45:57.810 --> 00:45:58.360
or longer.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:46:01.180
And then the other category
is purely muscular endurance,

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:03.450
which is essentially some
bout of exercise that's

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:04.890
going to be fairly
brief, anywhere

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:07.720
from a minute to 2 minutes but
certainly less than 12 minutes.

00:46:07.720 --> 00:46:09.720
OK, so let me give you
an example of an exercise

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:13.260
snack for enhancing your
long-duration endurance,

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:14.260
12 minutes or longer.

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:16.268
And this is the sort
of thing that if you

00:46:16.268 --> 00:46:18.060
are going to incorporate
into your routine,

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:19.650
and I highly
recommend that you do,

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:23.430
can essentially be done
any time with no warm-up.

00:46:23.430 --> 00:46:25.920
A good example of an
exercise snack of this type

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:28.890
would be to suddenly
stand up from your desk

00:46:28.890 --> 00:46:31.050
and to do 100 jumping jacks.

00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:33.050
Now, depending on
how fit you are

00:46:33.050 --> 00:46:35.450
and how fast you do
those jumping jacks

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:38.502
and how wide and tall you
do those jumping jacks,

00:46:38.502 --> 00:46:40.460
meaning are you doing
these little things where

00:46:40.460 --> 00:46:42.770
your hands don't actually meet
and your hands like parting

00:46:42.770 --> 00:46:44.120
your legs just a
little bit, or are you

00:46:44.120 --> 00:46:45.953
doing full jumping jacks
where you're really

00:46:45.953 --> 00:46:48.410
jumping and setting
your feet out

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:50.492
as wide as you
comfortably and safely can

00:46:50.492 --> 00:46:51.950
and then bring your
hands together,

00:46:51.950 --> 00:46:55.470
it could take you anywhere
from 30 seconds to 90 seconds.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:55.970
OK.

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:58.220
So in the case of
jumping jacks, you

00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:00.020
may end up doing
this for 90 seconds.

00:47:00.020 --> 00:47:02.840
But the point is to simply
do 100 jumping jacks.

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:04.850
Or if that takes too
long, you could even

00:47:04.850 --> 00:47:07.520
do just 25 or 50 jumping jacks.

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:10.440
The point is that it's going
to get you moving your muscles.

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:12.695
It's going to get
your heart rate up.

00:47:12.695 --> 00:47:15.320
Even if you're very, very fit if
you're doing these fast enough

00:47:15.320 --> 00:47:17.018
and you're doing them
with proper form,

00:47:17.018 --> 00:47:18.560
it's going to get
your heart rate up.

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:20.143
And then you're done.

00:47:20.143 --> 00:47:21.560
You can sit back
down to your desk

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:24.020
or you can continue to
walk through the airport.

00:47:24.020 --> 00:47:26.000
Yes, I've done these
in the airport.

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:29.000
Typically not while walking
toward my gate but at the gate.

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:30.650
But occasionally I'm
feeling lethargic,

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:33.110
or I haven't had the
opportunity to train that day,

00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:36.050
and perhaps I won't get
the opportunity to train,

00:47:36.050 --> 00:47:38.180
so I'll do something
like 100 jumping jacks

00:47:38.180 --> 00:47:40.430
while facing the
window so it feels

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:43.267
a little less awkward facing
people while you're doing them.

00:47:43.267 --> 00:47:45.350
And of course, you don't
have to do jumping jacks.

00:47:45.350 --> 00:47:47.930
And equally effective
type of exercise snack

00:47:47.930 --> 00:47:50.990
is to find a stairwell
and to simply

00:47:50.990 --> 00:47:53.330
go up that stairwell
as fast as you safely

00:47:53.330 --> 00:47:56.100
can for 20 to 30 seconds.

00:47:56.100 --> 00:47:58.310
So perhaps just find the
bottom of a stairwell

00:47:58.310 --> 00:48:00.340
and go up that stairwell
as quickly as you can.

00:48:00.340 --> 00:48:02.090
And perhaps go down
as quickly as you can.

00:48:02.090 --> 00:48:04.460
And just keep doing that
for about 20 to 40 seconds,

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:05.910
and then you're
essentially done.

00:48:05.910 --> 00:48:06.410
OK.

00:48:06.410 --> 00:48:09.950
You could also opt to
pick some distance away

00:48:09.950 --> 00:48:11.367
from your car in
the parking lot,

00:48:11.367 --> 00:48:13.700
assuming you're not carrying
any heavy bags or anything,

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:15.470
and simply run to your car.

00:48:15.470 --> 00:48:19.753
So 20 to 30 seconds of not
necessarily all out sprinting.

00:48:19.753 --> 00:48:21.170
You don't want to
injure yourself.

00:48:21.170 --> 00:48:23.090
Because again, this is
done without a warm-up.

00:48:23.090 --> 00:48:24.507
These exercise
snacks are designed

00:48:24.507 --> 00:48:27.170
to be inserted into your
day and into your week

00:48:27.170 --> 00:48:28.730
essentially at random.

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.210
You could plan them if you want.

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:32.727
But any time you feel inspired,
or perhaps any time you're

00:48:32.727 --> 00:48:34.310
feeling like you
don't want to do one,

00:48:34.310 --> 00:48:36.830
you could simply do one
of these exercise snacks.

00:48:36.830 --> 00:48:40.910
And of course, doing jumping
jacks, or running to your car,

00:48:40.910 --> 00:48:43.610
or taking the stairs
very quickly up and down,

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:45.560
or just up and
then walking down,

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:47.990
for instance, and doing a
few jumping jacks, things

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:50.870
of that sort, of course,
can take on a near infinite

00:48:50.870 --> 00:48:53.310
number of different variations.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:55.400
So if you don't like
any of the variations

00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:57.023
that I just presented,
you can easily

00:48:57.023 --> 00:48:58.190
come up with something else.

00:48:58.190 --> 00:49:00.710
Again, the purpose of
these exercise snacks

00:49:00.710 --> 00:49:02.370
is to get your heart rate up.

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:05.240
It's of course, to do this
while not getting injured,

00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:08.990
and it is entirely compatible
with an existing exercise

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:09.680
program.

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:13.580
It in no way is going to impede
your performance in strength

00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:16.130
or hypertrophy or other
forms of long-form endurance

00:49:16.130 --> 00:49:17.930
or high-intensity
interval training, quite

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:18.923
to the contrary.

00:49:18.923 --> 00:49:20.840
Everything we know about
these exercise snacks

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:23.630
is that they enhance various
aspects of your physiology

00:49:23.630 --> 00:49:26.570
in ways that promote
both recovery

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:29.510
and performance in your
other types of exercise

00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:31.590
and your other types
of athletic endeavors.

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:33.050
So no reason to think
that they are going to be

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:34.300
problematic for your training.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.740
But of course, don't
trip, don't fall,

00:49:36.740 --> 00:49:38.780
don't undertake a
movement that puts you

00:49:38.780 --> 00:49:41.330
into a range of motion that has
hurting your back, your knee,

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:42.680
or any other part of your body.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:44.877
Start off slowly
and find something

00:49:44.877 --> 00:49:45.960
that really works for you.

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:47.210
So these are very easy.

00:49:47.210 --> 00:49:49.520
They take very little time.

00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:51.240
They're fun to be honest.

00:49:51.240 --> 00:49:54.500
And as was discussed in the
conversation with Dr. Andy

00:49:54.500 --> 00:49:56.580
Galpin, they're
also very effective.

00:49:56.580 --> 00:49:58.430
The second category
of exercise snack

00:49:58.430 --> 00:50:02.060
are the exercise snacks that
enhance muscular endurance.

00:50:02.060 --> 00:50:04.550
So muscular endurance
is the type of endurance

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:07.010
that allows you to
maintain a fixed position

00:50:07.010 --> 00:50:10.680
for some period of time usually
somewhere between 1 and 3

00:50:10.680 --> 00:50:11.180
minutes.

00:50:11.180 --> 00:50:13.910
But these endurance
bouts are never

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:16.340
going to last 12 or
more minutes, unless you

00:50:16.340 --> 00:50:19.280
are exceptionally
evolved in terms

00:50:19.280 --> 00:50:21.050
of your muscular
endurance abilities.

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:23.270
What I'm talking about here
are things like planks,

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:27.470
wall sits, maximum number of
push-ups, things of that sort.

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:29.450
And this is an aspect
of fitness that

00:50:29.450 --> 00:50:32.060
translates to other
aspects of fitness

00:50:32.060 --> 00:50:33.547
in a very important way.

00:50:33.547 --> 00:50:35.630
And again, this was covered
in the exercise series

00:50:35.630 --> 00:50:36.820
with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:50:36.820 --> 00:50:38.570
I don't want to go
into any of the details

00:50:38.570 --> 00:50:40.670
now because it was
all covered there.

00:50:40.670 --> 00:50:43.160
It's very easy to look up
because it's time stamped.

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:47.240
But just being brief,
muscular endurance

00:50:47.240 --> 00:50:52.370
allows the buildup of more
microvascular supply to muscles

00:50:52.370 --> 00:50:54.650
and connective
tissue in a way that

00:50:54.650 --> 00:50:59.390
allows delivery and removal
of more nutrients and waste

00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:00.170
products.

00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:02.780
Now, I realize to the physios
things like nutrients and waste

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:04.530
products is far too
broad a category,

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:05.905
but what we're
talking about here

00:51:05.905 --> 00:51:08.960
is the ability to deliver
more fuel and oxygen

00:51:08.960 --> 00:51:13.400
and to remove waste
products of muscular effort,

00:51:13.400 --> 00:51:16.430
or to be more specific,
neuromuscular effort.

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:17.930
This is a great
thing because it can

00:51:17.930 --> 00:51:20.060
help you enhance your
strength training,

00:51:20.060 --> 00:51:21.530
enhance your
hypertrophy training,

00:51:21.530 --> 00:51:24.650
enhance your long-distance
endurance and your middle

00:51:24.650 --> 00:51:27.050
distance and even your
short distance endurance.

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:29.990
And these exercise snacks for
building muscular endurance

00:51:29.990 --> 00:51:31.993
are exceedingly easy to do.

00:51:31.993 --> 00:51:34.160
And you can even do them
while talking on the phone,

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:35.743
especially if you're
using headphones,

00:51:35.743 --> 00:51:37.660
or if you have your
phone on speaker.

00:51:37.660 --> 00:51:40.480
A good example of an exercise
snack for muscular endurance

00:51:40.480 --> 00:51:44.230
would be a 30 to 60-second
or perhaps longer wall sit.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:45.610
So remember wall sits?

00:51:45.610 --> 00:51:47.680
Wall sits are as
the name suggests

00:51:47.680 --> 00:51:50.170
where you put your feet out
some distance from a wall.

00:51:50.170 --> 00:51:51.880
You squat down into
a seated position,

00:51:51.880 --> 00:51:53.380
but there's no chair
there, and then

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:54.940
you maintain that
seated position,

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:57.908
and that is harder
to do over time.

00:51:57.908 --> 00:51:59.950
You could lean back against
the wall a little bit

00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:02.468
harder if you wanted to
gain some extra support

00:52:02.468 --> 00:52:03.010
and continue.

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:04.593
But the idea here
is that you're going

00:52:04.593 --> 00:52:06.580
to go not necessarily
to muscular failure

00:52:06.580 --> 00:52:08.980
but to the point where you
can't continue to sit in

00:52:08.980 --> 00:52:10.240
that wall sit position.

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:13.510
You could also simply do
this as an air squat down

00:52:13.510 --> 00:52:15.560
to the bottom position
where you're comfortable.

00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:17.507
And then you don't want
to rest down there,

00:52:17.507 --> 00:52:19.840
and you want to maintain some
tension in your quadriceps

00:52:19.840 --> 00:52:21.560
and other muscles
of your lower body

00:52:21.560 --> 00:52:24.100
so that you're actively
trying to support yourself

00:52:24.100 --> 00:52:27.430
in the seated position but
without a seat below you.

00:52:27.430 --> 00:52:30.940
That can be done again while
on a speakerphone conversation,

00:52:30.940 --> 00:52:33.400
that can be done at
random throughout the day.

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:35.110
You can just decide, OK, I'm
going to do a wall sit now,

00:52:35.110 --> 00:52:36.070
and I'm going to time myself.

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:37.990
I'm going to see how
long I can do this for.

00:52:37.990 --> 00:52:40.540
Or you could decide
to do a plank.

00:52:40.540 --> 00:52:42.107
I've done this while
on a phone call.

00:52:42.107 --> 00:52:43.690
Sorry if it was that
I was talking to,

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:45.280
but I've put the
phone on speaker

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:47.050
and just gotten into
a plank position,

00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:48.910
and then I'll just have the
conversation in the plank

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:49.570
position.

00:52:49.570 --> 00:52:52.810
I don't fight to maintain that
plank position past the point

00:52:52.810 --> 00:52:54.860
where I could continue
to have a conversation.

00:52:54.860 --> 00:52:56.680
So again, this is
a type of exercise

00:52:56.680 --> 00:52:58.090
that one is trying
to incorporate

00:52:58.090 --> 00:52:59.600
into their daily routine.

00:52:59.600 --> 00:53:02.710
If you wanted to dedicate
a specific amount of time

00:53:02.710 --> 00:53:04.930
just to doing these
exercise snacks, you could.

00:53:04.930 --> 00:53:06.550
But it's far more
reasonable to assume

00:53:06.550 --> 00:53:09.100
that people will incorporate
these into their daily routine

00:53:09.100 --> 00:53:11.440
more regularly if
you can incorporate

00:53:11.440 --> 00:53:14.650
it truly into the other
aspects your routine like work.

00:53:14.650 --> 00:53:17.590
You do this while watching
TV, or listening to a podcast.

00:53:17.590 --> 00:53:19.927
One form of muscular
endurance exercise snack

00:53:19.927 --> 00:53:22.510
that's really terrific and is a
bit of a challenge, that's fun

00:53:22.510 --> 00:53:25.450
is to just simply see how
many push-ups you can do.

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:28.900
And we talked about proper
push-up form during the episode

00:53:28.900 --> 00:53:32.630
series with Dr. Andy Galpin, but
here what we're talking about

00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:35.280
is chest all the
way to the ground

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:37.940
so it touches the ground, then
pushing up until your arms are

00:53:37.940 --> 00:53:38.570
completely straight.

00:53:38.570 --> 00:53:39.362
That's one push-up.

00:53:39.362 --> 00:53:42.110
And then continuing in
piston-like fashion,

00:53:42.110 --> 00:53:43.760
meaning you're not
pausing at the top

00:53:43.760 --> 00:53:45.122
and taking a bunch of breaths.

00:53:45.122 --> 00:53:47.330
You're not going into a
plank position in other words

00:53:47.330 --> 00:53:49.010
but continuing to
do as many push-ups

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:50.840
as you can to see
whether or not you can

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:52.700
enhance that number over time.

00:53:52.700 --> 00:53:55.940
And in any case, just
to simply get your body

00:53:55.940 --> 00:53:58.440
working to engage the muscles
of your chest, your shoulders,

00:53:58.440 --> 00:54:00.920
your triceps, and your
core, et cetera, and to

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:02.250
do that every once in a while.

00:54:02.250 --> 00:54:04.667
So instead of needing somebody
to say drop and give me 20,

00:54:04.667 --> 00:54:06.988
just see whether or not
at some point, any point

00:54:06.988 --> 00:54:09.280
throughout the day, you can
get into a push-up position

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:10.640
and do your maximum
number of push-ups,

00:54:10.640 --> 00:54:12.980
and then just mentally note
that number to yourself.

00:54:12.980 --> 00:54:15.722
Again, these exercise
snacks serve multiple roles.

00:54:15.722 --> 00:54:17.180
They're designed
to get you moving,

00:54:17.180 --> 00:54:20.602
to get your heart rate
going, to maintain or enhance

00:54:20.602 --> 00:54:22.310
your fitness in other
domains of fitness.

00:54:22.310 --> 00:54:25.892
And this is very important
to not take too much time out

00:54:25.892 --> 00:54:26.600
of your schedule.

00:54:26.600 --> 00:54:29.450
In fact, like zone
2 cardio, right,

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:31.340
being the type of
movement that you're just

00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:34.140
going to do a lot throughout
the week carrying groceries,

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:35.750
et cetera, as we
discussed earlier,

00:54:35.750 --> 00:54:37.280
these exercise
snacks are designed

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:39.530
to be incorporated
into your daily life.

00:54:39.530 --> 00:54:41.630
And I must say that
having started doing these

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:44.030
after recording the series
with Dr. Andy Galpin,

00:54:44.030 --> 00:54:45.150
I've noticed two things.

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:46.820
First of all, including
these exercise

00:54:46.820 --> 00:54:50.180
snacks at least once a week and
more like three to five times

00:54:50.180 --> 00:54:52.340
a week for me, so that's
one exercise snack

00:54:52.340 --> 00:54:56.480
done three to five times
per week has definitely

00:54:56.480 --> 00:54:58.910
correlated with
improvements in my fitness

00:54:58.910 --> 00:55:01.400
in other domains of fitness,
strength, hypertrophy,

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:02.980
long-distance
endurance, et cetera.

00:55:02.980 --> 00:55:04.730
Now, I've changed a
number of other things

00:55:04.730 --> 00:55:08.480
as well as a consequence of that
series with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:55:08.480 --> 00:55:12.298
So I can't say for sure that
it's the exercise snacks per se

00:55:12.298 --> 00:55:14.090
that are causing all
those positive shifts.

00:55:14.090 --> 00:55:16.640
I have to imagine that it's
not just the exercise snacks,

00:55:16.640 --> 00:55:18.928
but they've become an
important part of my routine

00:55:18.928 --> 00:55:20.720
and that relates to
the second point, which

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:24.420
is that the exercise snacks
are to be fun and easy.

00:55:24.420 --> 00:55:26.360
And so I really enjoy
them, so much so

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:28.310
that if I don't one
for couple of days,

00:55:28.310 --> 00:55:29.960
I start to crave
them a little bit

00:55:29.960 --> 00:55:31.550
like the other kind of snack.

00:55:31.550 --> 00:55:34.430
The next category of tool
to improve your fitness

00:55:34.430 --> 00:55:37.660
relates to breathing
or respiration.

00:55:37.660 --> 00:55:40.640
Now breathing and respiration
is an enormous topic

00:55:40.640 --> 00:55:41.760
in and of itself.

00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:45.170
In fact, I did an entire episode
on breathing and respiration,

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:48.080
and this is a topic that my
laboratory works on extensively

00:55:48.080 --> 00:55:51.440
as it relates to anxiety
control and some other aspects

00:55:51.440 --> 00:55:54.410
of mental health, as well
as to physical performance.

00:55:54.410 --> 00:55:57.290
For today's discussion, I want
to just review a few tools

00:55:57.290 --> 00:56:00.170
that one can incorporate
both into workouts

00:56:00.170 --> 00:56:02.630
and around workouts
that can greatly

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:05.150
enhance fitness and recovery.

00:56:05.150 --> 00:56:07.700
The first one is the
type of respiration tool

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:10.568
that you use between
sets of exercise.

00:56:10.568 --> 00:56:12.860
And again here we're talking
about resistance training,

00:56:12.860 --> 00:56:14.540
but we could just
as easily be talking

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:19.040
about rest between rounds of
say high-intensity interval

00:56:19.040 --> 00:56:19.800
training.

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:22.280
So for instance, between
bouts of sprinting

00:56:22.280 --> 00:56:24.890
on the track or the bike or
the treadmill or the rower.

00:56:24.890 --> 00:56:27.950
A great pattern of breathing
to incorporate during rest

00:56:27.950 --> 00:56:29.928
between sets is
something that I've

00:56:29.928 --> 00:56:31.720
talked about before in
other contexts which

00:56:31.720 --> 00:56:33.040
is the physiological side.

00:56:33.040 --> 00:56:37.300
The physiological side is a
deep Inhale through the nose

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:41.170
to maximally or near
maximally inflate your lungs,

00:56:41.170 --> 00:56:44.200
and then a second
very brief inhale,

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:46.810
and it's necessarily brief
because your lungs are already

00:56:46.810 --> 00:56:50.320
pretty full, to maximally
inflate your lungs

00:56:50.320 --> 00:56:52.210
and to make sure that
any of the little sacs

00:56:52.210 --> 00:56:53.668
or the little
alveoli of your lungs

00:56:53.668 --> 00:56:56.590
that have collapsed during
the exercise exertion

00:56:56.590 --> 00:56:58.240
will reinflate.

00:56:58.240 --> 00:57:01.790
And then a long exhale
until lungs empty.

00:57:01.790 --> 00:57:04.543
So I'll demonstrate it right now
as I've done many times before.

00:57:04.543 --> 00:57:06.460
But if you haven't seen
it or heard it before,

00:57:06.460 --> 00:57:09.338
it's two inhales followed
by an extended exhale.

00:57:09.338 --> 00:57:10.255
And it goes like this.

00:57:10.255 --> 00:57:13.462
[INHALE, EXHALE]

00:57:18.820 --> 00:57:21.060
You'll notice that the
inhales were through the nose

00:57:21.060 --> 00:57:22.960
and the exhale was
through the mouth.

00:57:22.960 --> 00:57:25.500
That's the ideal way to do
it for a number of reasons.

00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:28.020
Check out the episode that I
did on respiration physiology

00:57:28.020 --> 00:57:31.980
a.k.a. breathing if you want
more details on why that is.

00:57:31.980 --> 00:57:34.860
But two inhales through the
nose and a long extended exhale

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:37.590
through the mouth, the so-called
physiological side not named

00:57:37.590 --> 00:57:41.790
by me but rather named by
physiologists in the 1930

00:57:41.790 --> 00:57:46.080
is as far as we know,
the fastest way to shift

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:49.590
your nervous system from
so-called sympathetic drive

00:57:49.590 --> 00:57:52.020
to more parasympathetic
drive, from a state

00:57:52.020 --> 00:57:55.002
of greater alertness to
a state of greater calm.

00:57:55.002 --> 00:57:57.210
Now, the reason to do this
between sets of resistance

00:57:57.210 --> 00:58:00.090
training is that the more
that you can shift yourself

00:58:00.090 --> 00:58:02.460
from sympathetic drive to
parasympathetic drive, that

00:58:02.460 --> 00:58:05.910
is from alert to calm in
between sets, the more energy

00:58:05.910 --> 00:58:09.640
and focus you can devote to
exertion during your work sets.

00:58:09.640 --> 00:58:12.750
So one way to do this that's
very convenient and very

00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:16.260
effective is to consider
the last repetition

00:58:16.260 --> 00:58:19.810
of your set, a physiological
sigh, which is not

00:58:19.810 --> 00:58:21.320
to say, OK, I want
to be very clear,

00:58:21.320 --> 00:58:22.330
which is not to
say that you should

00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:24.280
do the physiological
side during your set.

00:58:24.280 --> 00:58:26.770
In fact, I recommend
that you do not do that.

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:29.470
But rather if you're doing
six repetitions of a given

00:58:29.470 --> 00:58:32.830
exercise and you, let's
say, fail on the sixth

00:58:32.830 --> 00:58:35.922
or you do that sixth repetition
and you're just close

00:58:35.922 --> 00:58:38.380
to failure because again your
work set should be to failure

00:58:38.380 --> 00:58:41.432
or close to it most of the
time, then set down the weight,

00:58:41.432 --> 00:58:43.390
and then you're going to
do the next repetition

00:58:43.390 --> 00:58:45.738
as the physiological
sigh, meaning you're not

00:58:45.738 --> 00:58:46.780
going to do the movement.

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:49.390
You're going to think of
doing a physiological sigh

00:58:49.390 --> 00:58:51.280
as the last repetition
of every set,

00:58:51.280 --> 00:58:54.100
not during the last repetition
of the resistance training

00:58:54.100 --> 00:58:55.090
movement, OK.

00:58:55.090 --> 00:58:56.740
So the physiological
sigh is something

00:58:56.740 --> 00:58:58.180
you do at the beginning
of the rest period

00:58:58.180 --> 00:58:59.500
immediately following a set.

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:00.830
If you'd rather think
about it that way

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:02.050
because it's more
convenient than thinking

00:59:02.050 --> 00:59:04.330
about it as the last rep
of a set, be my guest.

00:59:04.330 --> 00:59:05.410
Whatever works for you.

00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:07.600
But what you'll quickly
find is that if you

00:59:07.600 --> 00:59:09.670
do a physiological sigh
right after completing

00:59:09.670 --> 00:59:13.210
your last repetition, you'll
calm down much more quickly,

00:59:13.210 --> 00:59:15.310
your heart rate will
come down more quickly,

00:59:15.310 --> 00:59:17.710
and you'll recover more
completely in whatever

00:59:17.710 --> 00:59:19.858
designated rest period
you've allowed yourself,

00:59:19.858 --> 00:59:21.400
whether or not it's
30 seconds, which

00:59:21.400 --> 00:59:25.360
would be very short frankly, or
it's a 5-minute period of rest

00:59:25.360 --> 00:59:26.230
between sets.

00:59:26.230 --> 00:59:29.350
If you do one truly, just
one physiological sigh

00:59:29.350 --> 00:59:30.850
at the beginning
of the rest period,

00:59:30.850 --> 00:59:33.100
you are going to effectively
shift your nervous system

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:35.950
in the direction you want it to
go during those rest periods.

00:59:35.950 --> 00:59:38.242
And of course, if you're
training hard during your work

00:59:38.242 --> 00:59:40.540
sets you run zero
risk whatsoever

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:42.460
of feeling so calm
that you don't feel

00:59:42.460 --> 00:59:44.080
motivated to do your next set.

00:59:44.080 --> 00:59:46.780
I promise you that it will
allow you to relax more

00:59:46.780 --> 00:59:48.400
at the beginning
of the rest period

00:59:48.400 --> 00:59:51.610
than you ordinarily would, to
shift into a state of rest.

00:59:51.610 --> 00:59:54.070
There are differing opinions
about whether or not

00:59:54.070 --> 00:59:56.860
you should walk around or stay
still during your rest periods.

00:59:56.860 --> 00:59:59.500
I like to walk around a
bit and stay standing.

00:59:59.500 --> 01:00:01.390
I'm not one of these
people that collapses

01:00:01.390 --> 01:00:03.640
into a C-shape on the
bench in between sets.

01:00:03.640 --> 01:00:06.730
I like to stand up and breathe
normally and walk around, drink

01:00:06.730 --> 01:00:08.450
a little water, et cetera.

01:00:08.450 --> 01:00:10.790
In any case, doing
a physiological side

01:00:10.790 --> 01:00:12.790
the beginning of
each rest interval

01:00:12.790 --> 01:00:15.340
between work sets of
resistance training

01:00:15.340 --> 01:00:17.500
is a very effective
way to enhance

01:00:17.500 --> 01:00:20.350
your focus and your output
during your work sets.

01:00:20.350 --> 01:00:22.960
Now, the last respiration
tool to improve your fitness

01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:25.750
is again a tool gleaned from
the discussion with Dr. Andy

01:00:25.750 --> 01:00:29.990
Galpin, and that's
to include a 3 to 5

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:32.823
minute period at the end
of every single workout.

01:00:32.823 --> 01:00:34.990
So it doesn't matter if
it's high-intensity interval

01:00:34.990 --> 01:00:36.790
training, or it's
resistance training,

01:00:36.790 --> 01:00:41.110
or it's a long run of some sort,
at the end of every workout,

01:00:41.110 --> 01:00:44.110
to take 3 to 5 minutes, so
you'll want to set a timer,

01:00:44.110 --> 01:00:46.780
and to do some form of
parasympathetic, that

01:00:46.780 --> 01:00:49.750
is calming promoting
breathing in order

01:00:49.750 --> 01:00:52.900
to shift your nervous system
from a state of heightened

01:00:52.900 --> 01:00:55.990
alertness and output
into a state of recovery.

01:00:55.990 --> 01:00:58.150
Because as you all
well know by now,

01:00:58.150 --> 01:01:01.180
you get fitter not
during your workouts

01:01:01.180 --> 01:01:04.720
but rather after your
workouts, in between workouts.

01:01:04.720 --> 01:01:07.360
So you stimulate the
adaptation during a workout,

01:01:07.360 --> 01:01:10.330
but you get the adaptation,
you get the actual improvement

01:01:10.330 --> 01:01:11.530
in between workouts.

01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:13.900
And a common mistake that
many people make, and I

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:16.930
made this mistake for years,
was to finish a great workout,

01:01:16.930 --> 01:01:19.030
and then you're
texting on your phone,

01:01:19.030 --> 01:01:20.680
or you're talking on the
phone, or you're driving home.

01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:23.305
It's certainly not as intense as
the workout that you just did.

01:01:23.305 --> 01:01:25.930
Maybe you're even feeling really
calm from a nice long jog,

01:01:25.930 --> 01:01:28.077
or you had a particularly
good workout that day

01:01:28.077 --> 01:01:29.410
and you're feeling really happy.

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:33.010
So you're enjoying the
high, so to speak, well,

01:01:33.010 --> 01:01:35.500
when you do 3 to 5 minutes
of what's often called down

01:01:35.500 --> 01:01:38.260
regulation breathing
after a workout,

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:41.173
it allows you to
recover and to induce

01:01:41.173 --> 01:01:43.090
the adaptation that
you've been after, the one

01:01:43.090 --> 01:01:45.970
that you actually trained
for much more quickly.

01:01:45.970 --> 01:01:47.440
I can't tell you
how many people I

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:50.200
know who start to incorporate
this into their workouts,

01:01:50.200 --> 01:01:53.480
find that they recover far
better from their workouts,

01:01:53.480 --> 01:01:55.720
which might seem a
little bit surprising.

01:01:55.720 --> 01:01:58.180
Why would it be that just 3
to 5 minutes of some activity

01:01:58.180 --> 01:02:00.940
would enhance recovery
to such a great degree?

01:02:00.940 --> 01:02:02.650
And that's because
typically people

01:02:02.650 --> 01:02:04.347
don't bookend their workouts.

01:02:04.347 --> 01:02:06.430
They finish their workouts,
and of course, they're

01:02:06.430 --> 01:02:08.350
not continuing to
lift weights or run.

01:02:08.350 --> 01:02:10.420
But they move about
their day in their life

01:02:10.420 --> 01:02:13.630
even if it's preparing a meal in
a way that the level of stress,

01:02:13.630 --> 01:02:16.390
and therefore, stress
hormones, things like cortisol,

01:02:16.390 --> 01:02:18.130
adrenaline, things
that by the way

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:20.590
are excellent to elevate
during a workout, things

01:02:20.590 --> 01:02:22.660
like inflammatory
markers, which by the way,

01:02:22.660 --> 01:02:24.465
are great to enhance
during a workout.

01:02:24.465 --> 01:02:26.090
That actually happens
during a workout.

01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:28.630
You have a massive increase
in inflammatory markers, which

01:02:28.630 --> 01:02:31.540
might seem bad, but
all of those things

01:02:31.540 --> 01:02:34.540
are enhancing the adaptation
that you're seeking.

01:02:34.540 --> 01:02:36.400
But as soon as
those workouts end,

01:02:36.400 --> 01:02:38.410
you want to shift
into recovery mode.

01:02:38.410 --> 01:02:41.200
And this 3 to 5 minutes of
down regulation breathing

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:42.770
is a terrific way to do that.

01:02:42.770 --> 01:02:44.978
There are a couple of
different patterns of breathing

01:02:44.978 --> 01:02:48.980
that will work best, but all
of them emphasize exhales.

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:49.480
OK.

01:02:49.480 --> 01:02:51.730
I want to repeat that, all
of them emphasize exhales.

01:02:51.730 --> 01:02:53.740
So for instance, you
could just choose

01:02:53.740 --> 01:02:55.870
slow, deliberate breathing.

01:02:55.870 --> 01:02:57.430
How does that emphasize exhales?

01:02:57.430 --> 01:03:01.690
Well, ordinarily when we
breathe, we inhale actively

01:03:01.690 --> 01:03:03.370
and we exhale passively.

01:03:03.370 --> 01:03:05.920
Whenever we deliberately
breathe more slowly,

01:03:05.920 --> 01:03:08.050
we are actively exhaling.

01:03:08.050 --> 01:03:11.920
OK, so active exhales really
promote the calming response

01:03:11.920 --> 01:03:13.580
in brain and body.

01:03:13.580 --> 01:03:16.600
The other thing you could do
which many people are now doing

01:03:16.600 --> 01:03:20.460
is to do a repeated round
of physiological sighs.

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:22.150
So the double inhale
through the nose,

01:03:22.150 --> 01:03:26.340
long exhale through the mouth
but repeated for say 3 minutes.

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:27.640
That's another version.

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:31.860
The other thing you could do is
simply to notice your exhales

01:03:31.860 --> 01:03:35.550
and to emphasize your exhales,
make them longer and more

01:03:35.550 --> 01:03:36.903
vigorous than your inhales.

01:03:36.903 --> 01:03:39.570
Now, you don't want to turn this
into a breathwork session where

01:03:39.570 --> 01:03:40.040
you're--

01:03:40.040 --> 01:03:40.665
[INHALE,EXHALE]

01:03:40.665 --> 01:03:43.620
--you're doing it Pranayama
or Kundalini breathing

01:03:43.620 --> 01:03:44.710
or something of that sort.

01:03:44.710 --> 01:03:46.570
The idea is to calm down.

01:03:46.570 --> 01:03:48.540
So any time you're
extending your exhales,

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:50.190
you're actively
exhaling, you're trying

01:03:50.190 --> 01:03:52.000
to slow your breathing
down overall,

01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:54.250
you're going to shift yourself
in the right direction.

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:57.210
So rather than complicate
this type of tool,

01:03:57.210 --> 01:04:00.210
the best thing you can do is
just focus on those exhales,

01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:03.240
slow your breathing overall,
use physiological sighs

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:06.420
if you want, or simply
sit in your vehicle,

01:04:06.420 --> 01:04:10.080
or if you have to
drive home while doing

01:04:10.080 --> 01:04:12.110
this extended exhale
type of down regulation.

01:04:12.110 --> 01:04:13.860
Ideally you would take
a couple of minutes

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:17.190
and just shift your whole
system by not driving, closing

01:04:17.190 --> 01:04:19.780
your eyes and just sitting in
your car stationary, of course,

01:04:19.780 --> 01:04:22.210
don't drive with your eyes
closed, or bike with your eyes

01:04:22.210 --> 01:04:22.710
closed.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:26.500
Just simply calm down,
extend your exhales

01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:29.620
and shift from the
workout to the recovery

01:04:29.620 --> 01:04:32.350
mode, which is where the
progress is going to arrive.

